actor cp1 in methionine biosynthetic gene transcription</article-title><source>Mol. Cell Biol</source><year>1995</year><volume>15</volume><fpage>1879</fpage><lpage>1888</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7891681</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B29"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Piper</surname><given-names>MDW</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Reproducibility of oligonucleotide microarray transcriptome analyses. An interlaboratory comparison using chemostat cultures of saccharomyces cerevisiae</article-title><source>J. Biol. Chem</source><year>2002</year><volume>277</volume><fpage>37001</fpage><lpage>37008</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12121991</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B30"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Regenberg</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Growth-rate regulated genes have profound impact on interpretation of transcriptome profiling in saccharomyces cerevisiae</article-title><source>Genome Biol</source><year>2006</year><volume>7</volume><fpage>R107</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17105650</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B31"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Regnacq</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Sut1p interaction with cyc8p(ssn6p) relieves hypoxic genes from cyc8p-tup1p repression in saccharomyces cerevisiae</article-title><source>Mol. Microbiol</source><year>2001</year><volume>40</volume><fpage>1085</fpage><lpage>1096</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11401714</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B32"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Segal</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Module networks: identifying regulatory modules and their condition-specific regulators from gene expression data</article-title><source>Nat. Genet</source><year>2003</year><volume>34</volume><fpage>166</fpage><lpage>176</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12740579</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B33"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sosa</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Gcn4 negatively regulates expression of genes subjected to nitrogen catabolite repression</article-title><source>Biochem. Biophys. Res. Commun</source><year>2003</year><volume>310</volume><fpage>1175</fpage><lpage>1180</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14559239</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B34"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tai</surname><given-names>SL</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Two-dimensional transcriptome analysis in chemostat cultures. combinatorial effects of oxygen availability and macronutrient limitation in saccharomyces cerevisiae</article-title><source>J. Biol. Chem</source><year>2005</year><volume>280</volume><fpage>437</fpage><lpage>447</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15496405</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B35"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tan</surname><given-names>PK</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Evaluation of gene expression measurements from commercial microarray platforms</article-title><source>Nucleic Acids Res</source><year>2003</year><volume>31</volume><fpage>5676</fpage><lpage>5684</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14500831</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B36"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Weierstall</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Cloning and characterization of three genes (sut1-3) encoding glucose transporters of the yeast pichia stipitis</article-title><source>Mol. Microbiol</source><year>1999</year><volume>31</volume><fpage>871</fpage><lpage>883</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10048030</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B37"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wingender</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Transfac: an integrated system for gene expression regulation</article-title><source>Nucleic Acids Res</source><year>2000</year><volume>28</volume><fpage>316</fpage><lpage>319</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10592259</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B38"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yeang</surname><given-names>C-H</given-names></name><name><surname>Jaakkola</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Modeling the combinatorial functions of multiple transcription factors</article-title><source>J. Comput. Biol</source><year>2006</year><volume>13</volume><fpage>463</fpage><lpage>480</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16597252</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B39"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yu</surname><given-names>X</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Genome-wide prediction and characterization of interactions between transcription factors in saccharomyces cerevisiae</article-title><source>Nucleic Acids Res</source><year>2006</year><volume>34</volume><fpage>917</fpage><lpage>927</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16464824</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B40"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zitomer</surname><given-names>RS</given-names></name><name><surname>Lowry</surname><given-names>CV</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Regulation of gene expression by oxygen in saccharomyces cerevisiae</article-title><source>Microbiol Rev</source><year>1992</year><volume>56</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>11</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1579104</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list><app-group><app><title>APPENDIX</title><p>A detailed explanation for constraints <bold>c5</bold>, <bold>c8</bold> and <bold>c12</bold> is given.<disp-formula><graphic xlink:href="btn155um5"/></disp-formula><inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i33.jpg"/></inline-formula> is the inner product of row <italic>g</italic> from binary matrix <bold>T</bold> and column <italic>c</italic> from binary matrix <bold>M</bold><sup>+</sup> and indicates the number of active enhancers that binds a gene promoter. <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i34.jpg"/></inline-formula> indicates the number of active repressors in the promoter. The sum of these two terms is an integer between 0, when no active TFs bind the promoter, and <italic>F</italic>, when all TFs are activated and bind the promoter. The right side of constraint <bold>c5</bold> can be either 0, when both <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i35.jpg"/></inline-formula> and <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i36.jpg"/></inline-formula> are 0, or <italic>F</italic>, when one of the two equals 1. (Note that because of constraint <bold>c2</bold> the <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i37.jpg"/></inline-formula> coefficients cannot both be 1.) When the sum of the two inner products is zero, both <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i38.jpg"/></inline-formula> coefficients can be 0 or one of them can be 1, since 0&#x02264;0 and 0&#x02264;<italic>F</italic>. However, if there is at least one active enhancer or repressor that binds the promoter, i.e. the sum of the inner products, denoted by <italic>x</italic>, is positive, then one of the <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i39.jpg"/></inline-formula> coefficients must be 1, since <italic>x</italic>&#x02270;0 and only <italic>x</italic>&#x02264;<italic>F</italic> holds. Consequently, constraint <bold>c5</bold> forces a gene to be either upregulated or downregulated, when there is at least one active enhancer or one active repressor in the gene promoter.<disp-formula><graphic xlink:href="btn155um6"/></disp-formula>Constraint <bold>c6</bold> ensures that <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i40.jpg"/></inline-formula>, the strength of TF <italic>f</italic> under cultivation parameter <italic>c</italic>, is 0, when the <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i41.jpg"/></inline-formula> is 0, i.e. when <italic>f</italic> is not activated under <italic>c</italic>. In this case constraint <bold>c8</bold> becomes <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i42.jpg"/></inline-formula>, which is always satisfied, since <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i43.jpg"/></inline-formula>, the general enhancer strength of <italic>f</italic> is at most <italic>F</italic>. In the case that the TF is activated, i.e. <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i44.jpg"/></inline-formula> is 1, constraint <bold>c8</bold> becomes <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i45.jpg"/></inline-formula>. Together with constraint <bold>c7</bold>, which states that <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i46.jpg"/></inline-formula>, it forces <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i47.jpg"/></inline-formula> to be equal to <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i48.jpg"/></inline-formula> in the case that <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i49.jpg"/></inline-formula> is 1.<disp-formula><graphic xlink:href="btn155um7"/></disp-formula><inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i50.jpg"/></inline-formula> is the inner product of row <italic>g</italic> from binary matrix <bold>T</bold> and column <italic>c</italic> from continuous matrix <bold>S</bold><sup>+</sup> and indicates the sum of the strengths of all active enhancers that can bind the promoter of <italic>g</italic> under cultivation parameter <italic>c</italic>. <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i51.jpg"/></inline-formula> indicates the total repressor strength. From constraints <bold>c3</bold>, <bold>c4</bold> and <bold>c8</bold> we know that if there are no active TFs that can bind the promoter, both inner products as well as <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i52.jpg"/></inline-formula> are 0. In that case, constraint <bold>c12</bold> becomes 0&#x02265;&#x02212;<italic>F</italic><sup>2</sup> and is satisfied. In the case that there is at least one active enhancer or repressor that binds the promoter, the difference between the inner products can range from &#x02212;<italic>F</italic><sup>2</sup>, when all <italic>F</italic> TFs are active, bind the promoter and act as repressors with the maximal strength of <italic>F</italic>, to <italic>F</italic><sup>2</sup>, when the enhancer strength is at its maximum. If we want to call a gene upregulated, i.e. <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i53.jpg"/></inline-formula> is 1, than constraint <bold>c12</bold> becomes: <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i54.jpg"/></inline-formula>. Here, <italic>F</italic><sup>&#x02212;2</sup> plays the role of a small positive number. Consequently, a gene can only be upregulated, i.e. <inline-formula><inline-graphic xlink:href="btn155i55.jpg"/></inline-formula> can only be 1, when the enhancer strength is larger than the repressor strength.</p></app></app-group></back></article>